findings.failure_scenario:
MEASURED in a throwaway git worktree at the staged bytes. File written to ingest/src/main/java/binjava/ingest/TextBlockBlind.java:

    class TextBlockBlind {
      String usage() {
        return """
            pass \""" to quote a text block
            """;
      }
      String hostname() throws Exception {
        return Files.readString(Path.of("/etc/hostname")).trim();
      }
    }

`javac` compiles it clean (`\"""` is the standard way to embed three quotes in a text block -- the first quote is escaped so the three are not a terminator). Staged and run: `./scripts/check-io-seam.sh` printed `ok 1 business-logic file(s) take a seam for every clock, socket and store`, exit 0. `echo path | python3 scripts/io_seam_scan.py` printed `1` and no violation line. Printing code_only() on the file shows why: the text-block branch takes the FIRST `\"\"\"` after i+3, which is the escaped one; the scan resumes as code, immediately meets the REAL closing `\"\"\"` on the next line, treats it as a new text block, finds no terminator, and blanks to end of file -- taking Files.readString and Path.of with it. Everything after any such text block in any business-logic file is invisible.

Why this is not just 'deny-lists are incomplete': the diff asserts the opposite three times. scripts/io_seam_scan.py header -- 'One pass with a state variable cannot have that class of bug, because it never looks at a delimiter that is already inside something else.' IoSeamGateTest.aCHARLiteralHoldingAQuoteDoesNotBlindTheScanner javadoc -- 'A scanner that never looks at a delimiter already inside something else cannot have this class of bug at all, which is why the fix is a rewrite rather than a reorder.' The backlog cell -- 'a scanner that never looks at a delimiter already inside something else cannot have that class of bug at all'. The text-block branch DOES look at a delimiter that is inside an escape. The string and char branches handle `\\` correctly (`j += 2 if src[j] == '\\' else 1`); the text-block branch has no escape handling at all, and the case that pins text blocks (aBannedCallQUOTEDInACommentOrALiteralIsNotAViolation) uses a text block with no escaped quote, so it cannot see this.

The failure direction is the dangerous one: silent under-reporting over an unbounded span of a file, not a false refusal. Fix is inside the same function -- step through the text block character by character honouring `\\`, the way the string branch already does. Then pin it with the file above, which reds today.

MEASURED at the staged bytes. ingest/src/main/java/binjava/ingest/FpSeamStream.java:

    class FpSeamStream {
      private final BinStore store;
      FpSeamStream(BinStore store) { this.store = store; }
      InputStream segment(String key) throws Exception { return store.openStream(key); }
    }

`./scripts/check-io-seam.sh` -> `FAIL ... FpSeamStream.java:9: .openStream( reaches a socket directly`, exit 1. The receiver is the BinStore SEAM -- the exact shape code-structure.md rule 4 requires -- and the gate reports it as a direct socket. For the two entries whose literal starts with `.`, owner is '' so head is '' and the regex is `\s*(?:\.|::)\s*openStream\b`, which matches any receiver of any type.

This is the direction the change itself identifies as how a gate gets weakened rather than obeyed (anIdentifierENDINGInABannedNameIsNotAViolation's javadoc, and non-negotiable 2). The author fixed the owner-anchored half of it with `\b`; these two entries are unanchorable by construction because the owner is a java.net.URL variable. The sample that pins them, `samples.put(".openStream(", "Object t = u.openStream();")`, uses a bare receiver too, so the case ENCODES the over-broad behaviour instead of bounding it.

Not blocking because no method named openStream or openConnection exists in the tree today (git grep over all src/main and src/test finds only the two sample lines in IoSeamGateTest). It is close to live: the milestone in flight is segment serving ('proxy streams a segment through to many consumers, from one read'), and openStream is a natural name for the method that does it. Bounding it to a URL-typed receiver is not possible textually; the honest options are to type-qualify the entry (`URL.openStream` plus `URLConnection`-producing forms) or to record the false-refusal as a known shape beside the entry.
